                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "A drug gives a side effect of thirst. This would be a result\nof a drug that increased the SNS or decreased the PNS"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "A drug is contraindicated in asthmatic clients. This would be likely with drugs that decreased the SNS or increased the PNS due to the constriction of the airways.&nbsp;\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "A drug has a side effect of constipation. The drug would most likely be one that increases the SNS or decreases the PNS.\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "A drug gives your client a racing heart. This would be likely with drugs that increase the SNS or decrease the PNS\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No, this one is incorrect. Remember, while the actions of the sympathetic nervous system at the level of the urinary system seem to go all over the place with respect to constrictions and relaxations,&nbsp;<strong>the ultimate result is that we need to flee, not wee in a SNS response.&nbsp;</strong>&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Indeed, this one is incorrect. Remember, while the actions of the sympathetic nervous system at the level of the urinary system seem to go all over the place with respect to constrictions and relaxations,&nbsp;<strong>the ultimate result is that we need to flee, not wee in a SNS response.&nbsp;</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "tip": ""
                  },
                  "text": "A drug makes urination difficult. This would be likely with drugs that decrease the SNS or increase the PNS\n"
                }
